CORE
return_order1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(main\.c \|-> \(##1 main\.y == 1\)\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^\[main\.p1\] always \(!main\.c \|-> \(##1 main\.y == 0\)\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A function with two `return` statements, an earlier conditional
`return 4'd1` followed by an unconditional `return 4'd0`. Per IEEE
1800-2017 section 13.4.1 `return` terminates the function immediately, so
when the condition of the first `return` holds its value must be used and
the later `return` must not be reached; f(1) must be 1.

Observed: p0 is REFUTED because f(1) evaluates to 0.

Root cause: verilog_rtl_buildert::expand_function_call and
build_function_call in src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp merge the recorded
`return_states` in forward program order, so a later `return` overrides an
earlier one whose guard also holds. The analogous `break_states` loop in
build_for correctly merges in reverse program order ("These come in
program order, hence process in reverse order"); the `return_states` and
`continue_states` loops do not.
